<section class="section reveal" id="lists">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Lists: Ordered and Mutable Collections</h2>

  <p>A list is an ordered collection of items enclosed in square brackets. Lists are the most flexible collection type in Python — they can contain any mix of types, grow or shrink dynamically, and support powerful manipulation methods. Think of a list like a playlist on your phone: items have a specific order (index), you can add or remove songs, and you can rearrange them.</p>

  <p>Lists are mutable, meaning you can change their contents in place. This makes them efficient for building datasets incrementally, which is common in data preprocessing pipelines. However, this mutability also means you need to be careful about modifying lists inside functions or loops — unexpected side effects are a source of bugs.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A list is like a shopping list on paper. You can add items, cross them out, rearrange the order, and see exactly how many items you have. Tuples (which we will see next) are like a recipe ingredient list that is printed in a cookbook — you can read it, but you cannot change it. Sets are like a unique list where no item can appear twice — useful for tracking membership.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Creating lists</span>
<span class="va">features</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>]  <span class="cm"># int list</span>
<span class="va">labels</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="st">"cat"</span>, <span class="st">"dog"</span>, <span class="st">"bird"</span>]
<span class="va">mixed</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="st">"text"</span>, <span class="nm">3.14</span>, <span class="kw">True</span>]
<span class="va">empty</span> <span class="op">=</span> []

<span class="cm"># Accessing elements by index (0-based)</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">features</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>])     <span class="cm"># 1</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">features</span>[<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>])    <span class="cm"># 3 — last element</span>

<span class="cm"># Modifying lists</span>
<span class="va">features</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">10</span>      <span class="cm"># change first element</span>
<span class="va">features</span>.<span class="fn">append</span>(<span class="nm">4</span>)    <span class="cm"># add to end</span>
<span class="va">features</span>.<span class="fn">extend</span>([<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>])  <span class="cm"># add multiple</span>
<span class="va">features</span>.<span class="fn">insert</span>(<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">99</span>)  <span class="cm"># insert at index 1</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We create lists with different types of elements. Lists use zero-based indexing, so the first element is at index 0. Negative indices count from the end: index -1 is the last element, -2 is second-to-last, etc. We modify the list using common methods: <code>append()</code> adds one item, <code>extend()</code> adds multiple items, and <code>insert()</code> adds at a specific position.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Essential List Methods for AI</h3>
  <p>Lists have many useful methods. The most important for AI work are: <code>append()</code> for adding items, <code>remove()</code> for deleting by value, <code>sort()</code> for ordering, <code>len()</code> for counting, and slicing with <code>[start:stop:step]</code> for extracting subsequences.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Slicing lists</span>
<span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="nm">85</span>, <span class="nm">90</span>, <span class="nm">78</span>, <span class="nm">95</span>, <span class="nm">88</span>]
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>:<span class="nm">4</span>])      <span class="cm"># [90, 78, 95] — index 1 to 3</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>[:<span class="nm">2</span>])       <span class="cm"># [85, 90] — first 2</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>[<span class="nm">2</span>:])       <span class="cm"># [78, 95, 88] — from index 2 on</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>[::2])      <span class="cm"># [85, 78, 88] — every 2nd item</span>

<span class="cm"># Common operations</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>))          <span class="cm"># 5</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">max</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>))         <span class="cm"># 95</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">min</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>))         <span class="cm"># 78</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>))         <span class="cm"># 436</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We demonstrate list slicing, which is a fundamental technique in data processing. The syntax <code>[start:stop:step]</code> extracts a portion of the list. Note that the stop index is exclusive (not included). We also show built-in functions like <code>len()</code>, <code>max()</code>, <code>min()</code>, and <code>sum()</code>, which operate on any sequence.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Lists are ordered, mutable sequences containing any types of elements</li>
      <li>Use zero-based indexing and negative indices to access from the end</li>
      <li>Use slicing <code>[start:stop:step]</code> to extract subsequences</li>
      <li>Essential methods: <code>append()</code>, <code>extend()</code>, <code>remove()</code>, <code>sort()</code></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="1">
      <p>What does <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5][1:4]</code> return?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) [1, 2, 3]</li>
        <li>B) [2, 3, 4]</li>
        <li>C) [2, 3, 4, 5]</li>
        <li>D) [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">Slicing [start:stop] includes index start but excludes index stop. So [1:4] returns elements at indices 1, 2, and 3, which is [2, 3, 4].</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

