<section class="section reveal" id="tuples">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Tuples: Immutable and Hashable</h2>

  <p>A tuple is an ordered collection enclosed in parentheses. Unlike lists, tuples are immutable — you cannot change, add, or remove elements after creation. This immutability makes tuples safer for protecting data and faster for lookup operations. Tuples are also hashable, meaning they can be used as dictionary keys or stored in sets, unlike lists.</p>

  <p>In AI work, tuples are useful for fixed data like image dimensions (height, width, channels), model shape specifications, or return values from functions. They are also used extensively in pandas for indexing and in NumPy for array shapes.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Creating tuples</span>
<span class="va">dimensions</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="nm">28</span>, <span class="nm">28</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>)  <span class="cm"># image shape</span>
<span class="va">coords</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">20</span>)              <span class="cm"># 2D coordinates</span>
<span class="va">single</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="nm">42</span>,)                <span class="cm"># note the comma!</span>
<span class="va">empty</span> <span class="op">=</span> ()

<span class="cm"># Accessing elements (same as lists)</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">dimensions</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>])    <span class="cm"># 28</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">dimensions</span>[<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>])   <span class="cm"># 1</span>

<span class="cm"># Immutability — this will raise an error</span>
<span class="cm"># dimensions[0] = 32  # TypeError!  Cannot assign to tuple</span>

<span class="cm"># Use tuples as dictionary keys</span>
<span class="va">image_cache</span> <span class="op">=</span> {}
<span class="va">image_cache</span>[<span class="va">dimensions</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"image_data"</span>  <span class="cm"># tuples are hashable</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We create tuples for fixed data structures common in AI. Note the critical syntax: a single-element tuple requires a trailing comma <code>(42,)</code> — without it, <code>(42)</code> is just a number in parentheses. We show that tuples are immutable (trying to modify raises TypeError) but can be used as dictionary keys because they are hashable.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Tuples are ordered, immutable sequences enclosed in parentheses</li>
      <li>Use tuples for fixed data like shapes, dimensions, and function returns</li>
      <li>Tuples are hashable — they can be dictionary keys or set members</li>
      <li>Immutability prevents accidental modification and enables optimizations</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="section-divider">

<section class="section reveal" id="dictionaries">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Dictionaries: Key-Value Storage</h2>

  <p>A dictionary stores data as key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces. Dictionaries are unordered (in Python 3.7+, they maintain insertion order for convenience), mutable, and incredibly efficient for lookups. Any hashable object can be a key — strings, numbers, tuples — but lists and dictionaries cannot (they are not hashable).</p>

  <p>Dictionaries are fundamental in data processing pipelines. You use them to map feature names to their indices, store configuration parameters, parse JSON data from APIs, and cache results. They are also the foundation of Pandas DataFrames under the hood.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A dictionary is like a phonebook or contact list: you look up a person's name (the key) to find their phone number (the value). With a list, you would have to scan through every entry until you find the right name. With a dictionary, the lookup is instantaneous.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Creating dictionaries</span>
<span class="va">config</span> <span class="op">=</span> {<span class="st">"learning_rate"</span>: <span class="nm">0.001</span>, <span class="st">"batch_size"</span>: <span class="nm">32</span>}
<span class="va">model_params</span> <span class="op">=</span> {<span class="st">"layers"</span>: <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="st">"activation"</span>: <span class="st">"relu"</span>}
<span class="va">empty_dict</span> <span class="op">=</span> {}

<span class="cm"># Accessing values</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">config</span>[<span class="st">"learning_rate"</span>])  <span class="cm"># 0.001</span>

<span class="cm"># Adding and modifying</span>
<span class="va">config</span>[<span class="st">"epochs"</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">100</span>  <span class="cm"># add new key</span>
<span class="va">config</span>[<span class="st">"learning_rate"</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.01</span>  <span class="cm"># modify</span>

<span class="cm"># Safe access with .get()</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">config</span>.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="st">"dropout"</span>, <span class="nm">0.5</span>))  <span class="cm"># returns 0.5 if missing</span>

<span class="cm"># Iterate over keys and values</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">key</span>, <span class="va">value</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">config</span>.<span class="fn">items</span>():
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"</span>{<span class="va">key</span>}<span class="st">: </span>{<span class="va">value</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We create dictionaries for storing configuration and parameters. Accessing with <code>config[key]</code> raises KeyError if the key doesn't exist. The safer <code>.get(key, default)</code> method returns the default value if missing. We iterate over key-value pairs using <code>.items()</code>, which is the standard pattern for processing dictionaries.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Data Processing:</strong> JSON APIs return dictionaries. CSV data is loaded into dictionaries before conversion to Pandas. Machine learning configurations are dictionaries. Mastering dictionaries is essential for working with real-world data.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Dictionaries map keys to values using <code>{key: value}</code> syntax</li>
      <li>Keys must be hashable (str, int, tuple); values can be any type</li>
      <li>Use <code>.get(key, default)</code> for safe access; raises KeyError otherwise</li>
      <li>Iterate with <code>.items()</code>, <code>.keys()</code>, or <code>.values()</code></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>Test Your Understanding</h4>
    <div class="quiz-question" data-correct="2">
      <p>What does <code>{"a": 1}.get("b", 0)</code> return?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) KeyError: 'b'</li>
        <li>B) None</li>
        <li>C) 0</li>
        <li>D) "b"</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">The <code>.get()</code> method safely returns a default value (0) if the key ('b') is not found, instead of raising an error.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

