<section class="section reveal" id="sets">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Sets: Unique Elements and Fast Membership Tests</h2>

  <p>A set is an unordered collection of unique elements. Sets are extremely useful for deduplication, membership testing (is this item in the set?), and mathematical operations like union and intersection. Sets cannot contain duplicates — if you add the same element twice, it appears only once.</p>

  <p>In AI, sets are useful for finding unique class labels, removing duplicate samples, tracking which IDs have been processed, and checking if a value exists in a collection (much faster than checking a list).</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Creating sets</span>
<span class="va">classes</span> <span class="op">=</span> {<span class="st">"cat"</span>, <span class="st">"dog"</span>, <span class="st">"bird"</span>}
<span class="va">unique_ids</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">set</span>([<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>])  <span class="cm"># {1, 2, 3}</span>
<span class="va">empty_set</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">set</span>()  <span class="cm"># note: {} is empty dict, not set</span>

<span class="cm"># Membership testing (very fast)</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> <span class="st">"cat"</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">classes</span>:
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Cat found"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Set operations</span>
<span class="va">set_a</span> <span class="op">=</span> {<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>}
<span class="va">set_b</span> <span class="op">=</span> {<span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>}
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">set_a</span> <span class="op">|</span> <span class="va">set_b</span>)  <span class="cm"># {1, 2, 3, 4} — union</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">set_a</span> <span class="op">&</span> <span class="va">set_b</span>)  <span class="cm"># {2, 3} — intersection</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">set_a</span> <span class="op">-</span> <span class="va">set_b</span>)  <span class="cm"># {1} — difference</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We create sets using curly braces or the <code>set()</code> function. Note that <code>{}</code> creates an empty dictionary; use <code>set()</code> for an empty set. Sets automatically remove duplicates: converting <code>[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]</code> to a set yields <code>{1, 2, 3}</code>. Membership testing with <code>in</code> is O(1) on average, making it much faster than checking a list. We show set operations (union |, intersection &, difference -) which are useful for comparing collections.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Sets are unordered, unique collections of hashable elements</li>
      <li>Use <code>set()</code> to create an empty set, not <code>{}</code></li>
      <li>Membership testing (<code>in</code>) is O(1) — much faster than lists</li>
      <li>Set operations: union |, intersection &, difference -, symmetric_difference ^</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

