<section class="section reveal" id="choosing-collections">
  <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
  <h2>Choosing the Right Collection</h2>

  <p>Each collection type has strengths and trade-offs. Lists are flexible and ordered but slower for membership testing. Tuples are immutable and hashable but fixed-size. Dictionaries have fast lookups by key but maintain insertion order (Python 3.7+). Sets are fast for membership and deduplication but unordered and cannot contain duplicates.</p>

  <p>Choosing the right collection type is crucial for both code correctness and performance. A list is appropriate when you need to maintain order and modify items. A tuple is better when you need immutability and want to use the collection as a dictionary key or set element. A dictionary is essential when you need fast lookups by arbitrary keys. A set is the perfect choice when you need to test membership quickly or remove duplicates.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Collection Types Comprehensive Comparison</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Lists</h5>
        <p>Ordered, mutable, flexible.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use for: dynamic data, sequences, stacks</li>
          <li>Lookup by index: O(1) fast</li>
          <li>Membership test: O(n) slow</li>
          <li>Hashable: No</li>
          <li>Common methods: append, extend, insert, remove, pop, sort</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Tuples</h5>
        <p>Ordered, immutable, fixed.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use for: fixed data, dictionary keys, function returns</li>
          <li>Lookup by index: O(1) fast</li>
          <li>Membership test: O(n) slow</li>
          <li>Hashable: Yes</li>
          <li>Cannot be modified after creation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Dictionaries</h5>
        <p>Key-value pairs, fast lookup.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use for: mappings, configs, caches, lookups</li>
          <li>Lookup by key: O(1) fast (average)</li>
          <li>Iteration: O(n)</li>
          <li>Hashable: No (but keys must be)</li>
          <li>Maintains insertion order (Python 3.7+)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Sets</h5>
        <p>Unique elements, no order.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Use for: deduplication, membership, set operations</li>
          <li>Membership test: O(1) fast (average)</li>
          <li>Add/Remove: O(1) fast (average)</li>
          <li>Hashable: No (elements must be)</li>
          <li>Common operations: union, intersection, difference</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Performance Considerations and Use Cases</h3>
  <p>When processing large datasets in AI applications, collection performance matters. If you need to frequently check whether a value exists in your collection, use a set (O(1)) rather than a list (O(n)). If you need fast lookups by key, always use a dictionary instead of iterating through lists of tuples. For fixed-size data like image dimensions or coordinates, tuples are both more semantically appropriate and prevent accidental modification.</p>

  <p>In machine learning pipelines, you will often mix collection types: lists for sequences of samples, dictionaries for mapping feature names to indices, tuples for fixed shape specifications, and sets for tracking processed IDs or unique classes. Understanding how to combine these effectively is essential.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Real-world example: combining collection types</span>
<span class="va">dataset</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"features"</span>: [[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>], [<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>], [<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]],  <span class="cm"># list of lists</span>
    <span class="st">"labels"</span>: [<span class="st">"A"</span>, <span class="st">"B"</span>, <span class="st">"A"</span>],           <span class="cm"># list of strings</span>
    <span class="st">"shape"</span>: (<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>),                      <span class="cm"># tuple (immutable)</span>
    <span class="st">"classes"</span>: {<span class="st">"A"</span>, <span class="st">"B"</span>}              <span class="cm"># set (unique values)</span>
}

<span class="cm"># Fast lookup of feature names</span>
<span class="va">feature_index</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"age"</span>: <span class="nm">0</span>,
    <span class="st">"income"</span>: <span class="nm">1</span>,
    <span class="st">"credit_score"</span>: <span class="nm">2</span>
}  <span class="cm"># O(1) lookup instead of O(n)</span>

<span class="cm"># Track which samples have been processed</span>
<span class="va">processed_ids</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">set</span>()  <span class="cm"># fast membership test</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">sample_id</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">new_ids</span>:
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">sample_id</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">processed_ids</span>:  <span class="cm"># O(1) check</span>
        <span class="va">processed_ids</span>.<span class="fn">add</span>(<span class="va">sample_id</span>)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We demonstrate a realistic scenario combining all four collection types. The dictionary stores a dataset with mixed structures. The feature_index dictionary enables O(1) feature lookup instead of searching through a list. The set efficiently tracks processed IDs with constant-time membership testing. This pattern is common in real ML pipelines for efficient data handling.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Data Processing:</strong> Using the right collection type can mean the difference between a pipeline that runs in seconds versus one that runs for hours. When processing millions of samples, the O(1) lookup of a dictionary or set can save hours of computation compared to O(n) list searches.</p>
    <p><strong>In Code Clarity:</strong> Using the appropriate collection type also makes your code more readable. Someone reading <code>processed_ids.add(id)</code> immediately understands you are tracking unique IDs. Using a list would be semantically incorrect and confusing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>List: ordered, mutable, flexible; O(1) index lookup, O(n) membership; use for sequences</li>
      <li>Tuple: ordered, immutable, hashable; O(1) index lookup; use for fixed data and keys</li>
      <li>Dictionary: key-value pairs, O(1) average key lookup; use for mappings and fast access</li>
      <li>Set: unique elements, O(1) average membership; use for deduplication and set operations</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

