<section class="section reveal" id="nested-collections">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Patterns</span>
  <h2>Nested Collections and Real-World Patterns</h2>

  <p>In real AI projects, you rarely work with simple collections. Instead, you combine them: lists of dictionaries (common for structured data), dictionaries of lists (common for datasets), and tuples as keys (for caching). Understanding how to work with nested structures is essential for data processing.</p>

  <p>Pandas DataFrames internally use dictionaries of Series (which are list-like), so understanding how to manipulate nested Python collections helps you work effectively with Pandas. Similarly, JSON data from APIs arrives as nested dictionaries and lists that you need to parse and manipulate.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># List of dictionaries — common data format</span>
<span class="va">students</span> <span class="op">=</span> [
    {<span class="st">"name"</span>: <span class="st">"Alice"</span>, <span class="st">"grade"</span>: <span class="nm">95</span>, <span class="st">"courses"</span>: [<span class="st">"ML"</span>, <span class="st">"DL"</span>]},
    {<span class="st">"name"</span>: <span class="st">"Bob"</span>, <span class="st">"grade"</span>: <span class="nm">87</span>, <span class="st">"courses"</span>: [<span class="st">"ML"</span>, <span class="st">"NLP"</span>]},
]

<span class="cm"># Access nested data</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">students</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>][<span class="st">"name"</span>])  <span class="cm"># Alice</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">students</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>][<span class="st">"courses"</span>][<span class="nm">0</span>])  <span class="cm"># ML</span>

<span class="cm"># Dictionary of lists — common for multi-dimensional data</span>
<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"age"</span>: [<span class="nm">25</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>, <span class="nm">35</span>],
    <span class="st">"income"</span>: [<span class="nm">50000</span>, <span class="nm">60000</span>, <span class="nm">75000</span>],
    <span class="st">"employed"</span>: [<span class="kw">True</span>, <span class="kw">True</span>, <span class="kw">False</span>]
}

<span class="cm"># Iterate through columns efficiently</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">column_name</span>, <span class="va">column_values</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">data</span>.<span class="fn">items</span>():
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"</span>{<span class="va">column_name</span>}<span class="st">: </span>{<span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">column_values</span>)}<span class="st"> samples"</span>)</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We show two fundamental patterns. The list of dictionaries is how most structured data arrives from APIs and databases — each dictionary represents a record with named fields. The dictionary of lists is the column-oriented format used internally by Pandas and excellent for statistical operations. Being able to convert between these formats and navigate them is essential.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>List of dictionaries: each element is a record with named fields (row-oriented)</li>
      <li>Dictionary of lists: each value is a column of data (column-oriented)</li>
      <li>Use appropriate indexing: <code>data[row][column]</code> for list-of-dicts, <code>data[column][row]</code> for dict-of-lists</li>
      <li>Understand these patterns to transition smoothly to Pandas and NumPy</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

